--- Part 3: fraction of looking-down rays with shipped VoL == 0 ---
grid: sun elev {90,70,45,20,5} x ray elev -85..-5 step 5 x azim 0..345 step 15
  samples                    : 2040
  shipped VoL == 0           : 1791  (87.8%)
  candidate VoL  > 0         : 1791  (87.8%)

Facts established (all re-derivable from the printout above):
1. shipped  = (eta*sx, +sqrt(k), eta*sz): the unit vector pointing AT THE SUN
   AS SEEN FROM UNDERWATER (apparent sun). Its elevation obeys Snell
   (sin(theta_t) = sin(theta_i)/1.2): 60deg -> 65.3757 deg elevation.
2. candidate = -(shipped): the propagation direction of sunlight inside the
   water (downward, anti-solar in xz).
3. Looking down at the seabed with the shipped axis gives VoL = 0 at every
   hour, because dot(ray_down, apparent_sun_up) <= 0. The gate at
   light_shafts.glsl:75 then returns before the march, so noon seabed pixels
   carried no shafts at all -- the pre-fix shipped state.
4. The candidate axis puts the looking-down hemisphere on the positive side:
   VoL > 0 for seabed rays, VoL = 0 when looking up at the surface.

Choose the candidate, as the work order directs. Recorded caveat for the
in-game pass (checklist 10b): because dot(ray, propagation) is the BACKWARD
scattering cosine, 'looking up at the surface' (the textbook forward-scatter
direction) now returns VoL = 0. Both directions cannot be positive at once
with a single cosine gate; the acceptance criterion for this fix is the
work order's own: noon seabed shows beams, looking up does not.
